  "essay": "In many countries, transport has become a serious problem. Some people believe that governments should spend money on improving public transport rather than building new roads. I partly agree with this view: public transport deserves the larger share of investment, but new roads are still necessary in some situations.\n\nThe first reason to prioritise public transport is that many people do not own a car and depend on buses and trains to get to work or school. If buses were more reliable and comfortable, more people would use them, and traffic congestion would decrease. In my city, buses are often late and extremely crowded, so many residents avoid them, while train fares are too high for students. Governments should therefore make public transport cheaper, faster and more dependable.\n\nHowever, building new roads also matters. Cities are growing and more people buy cars every year. Without new roads, traffic jams would become severe and people would waste hours commuting. For example, my father drives to work every day and spends two hours on the road, which harms both his well-being and the economy. In such cases, additional roads can ease pressure on the network.\n\nNevertheless, I believe public transport is more important overall. New roads tend to encourage more car use and therefore more pollution, whereas a single bus or train can carry many passengers at once and is better for the environment. If more people took public transport, cities would be quieter, the air would be cleaner, and everyone would be healthier and happier.\n\nIn conclusion, although new roads are sometimes necessary, I believe governments should allocate more funding to public transport, while building roads only where they are genuinely needed. Both are important for people and for the development of the country.",
